Z||000005||Formula88||11-04-2001||10:34 PM||||Check Ogre's Cave (link at bottom of page). He has an article on adding cooling fans to the engine compartment.  Not a bad idea, but the hood scoop may do just as good a job, without any fans.  One other thing you can do, if you have the stamped steel engine louvers (not the ones with the honeycomb beneath the louvre) - you can remove the stamped steel lower part to let more heat out.  Those vents aren't as free flowing.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Turkey on Rye||11-04-2001||10:58 PM||||ok, thanx alot man, I'll check it out.||||reg||1
Z||000007||falconhulk||11-05-2001||09:32 AM||||I wired 2 fans out of heavy duty servers to my grills.  I sealed the only opening (small hole on top with vinyl caulk.  I attached the fan to the underside of the grill screen mesh with wire ties.  I then bought a electric adjustable thermostat from JC whitnry for $13.  I put the probe from the thermo(normally suppose to attach to the back of the radiator) just under the manifold.  I then ran the car in traffic until it got to the max temp that it normally got to, 225 and set the thermostat to come on just under there.  So it comes on at about 200 now.  After being in traffic it will run when the car is shut off but only for about 7 minutes.  The fans only draw .8 amps a piece and really move the hot air out of the compartment. Under normal conditions my temp really does not go higher then around 180.  I actually just did this becasue I thought it was a cool idea and had the fans.  These fans are made to take constant operation for years as they are on all the time in the servers.  My only worry was weather proofing them.  I never have my car out of the garage anyway but I sealed it VERY good and I have turned them on and washed the car to see if there would be a problem.  I also wired a 10 amp in line fuse in case there was.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 11-05-2001).]||||reg||1
